#--: Introduction

The compiler, #~"zc", turns a program's units into C and hands the C to a C
compiler. Its stages run in this order, each taking what the one before it
gives:

#ol:
  [#*: the [#> to=virtual-file-system: Virtual File System] presents the
    source roots as one tree;]
  [#*: the [#> to=tokenizer: Tokenizer] and the [#> to=lexer: Lexer] turn a
    unit's text into tokens;]
  [#*: the [#> to=parser: Parser] builds the syntax tree of every unit the
    program names;]
  [#*: [#> to=generator-desugaring: Generator Desugaring] prepares each
    generator for the checker;]
  [#*: the [#> to=semantic-analyser: Semantic Analyser] checks types,
    ownership and locks, and records what it finds;]
  [#*: the [#> to=code-generator: Code Generator] writes the C.]

Each section below says what its stage takes and gives, and links to the
page that owns its detail. [#> to=./zc.html: zc] gives the command line.

#--: Virtual File System

A simple readonly Virtual File System (VFS) is used to present a unified source
tree to the compiler.

The VFS allows union or bind mounts to mount one filesystem over another. The
VFS tree presented to the compiler is usually the system directory with the
user source directory union mounted over it, so a user unit is found first
and shadows a system unit of the same name.

The VFS allows abstraction over a source tree that may be arbitrarily complex
while providing a simple interface to the compiler. The VFS tree can be built
from multiple physical filesystems or can be built from alternate data stores
(such as databases or remote systems).

#--: Tokenizer

The Tokenizer splits a single top level unit aquired from the VFS (usually
represented by a file) into a stream of tokens. Every part of the source
becomes part of a token and so the original source could be recreated by
concatenating all of the tokens returned by the tokenizer.

The tokenizer splits the file into tokens such as:

#ul:
  #*: horizontal whitespace (spaces and tabs only)
  #*: end of line
  #*: comments
  #*: identifiers
  #*: keywords
  #*: delimiters such as #~"(", #~")", #~"{" and #~"}"
  #*: dot operator: #~"."
  #*: parts of string literals (raw and interpolated)
  #*: numeric literals
  #*: error tokens

The tokens have a type, the string contents and an identifier for the file that
they are contained within and the location (line and column) within that source
file.

The [#> to=./stdlib_tokenize.html: tokenize] unit gives the tokenizer and
the lexer to programs; its page owns their detail.

#--: Lexer

The Lexer provides another layer over the Tokenizer to ease the job of the
parser which will follow.

The Lexer filters comments and whitespace as these are not used by the parser.
It also keeps a small stack to maintain context for handling end-of-line (EOL).

In Blocks (the top level of a unit or a brace delimitered block), multiple
EOL's are coalesced into a single EOL token. In parenthesized expressions, all
EOL's are ignored. In string literals, all EOL's are passed through as-is and
form part of that string literal.

The Lexer buffers one token of lookahead, and never copies it out. A rule that
wants to know what comes next asks #~"peekType" for the token type or
#~"peekPos" for a #~"tokpos" -- a valtype spanning the token: fsno, line,
column, and the text's byte width, which is everything a diagnostic needs from
the text without the text itself. A rule that must read the characters asks
#~"peekTextId" for the text's id in the name pool, where every token's text is
interned.

Everything the Lexer hands out copies out: a #~"tokpos" and a pool id are both
plain values, so a rule can capture where its construct began, or what it was
called, and still use it a hundred lines and several #~"advance" calls later.
Consuming a token is free: #~"accept" / #~"acceptAny" #_+"move" the buffered
token out rather than duplicating it.

#--: Parser

The parser creates an abstract syntax tree (AST) from the source units
supplied. The [#> to=./stdlib_ast.html: ast] unit gives the parser and the
tree to programs; its page owns the tree's shape. It begins with the 'core' system unit and the user 'main' unit and
parses each, loading further units as they are referred to.

The parser resolves no names. After a unit is parsed, one walk over its body
(#~"collectExterns") collects the identifiers the unit refers to but does not
bind -- biased to over-collect, since a spurious name simply fails to name a
file -- and for each such name the VFS is asked for #~"<name>.z": first in the
directory named after the current unit and lying beside its file (an
implementation subunit), for every name and a core name too, then in the
source tree and the system library. A unit found this way is parsed the
same way, recursively, until the worklist is empty. Whether a name actually
denotes a unit, a type, or a variable is the type checker's question, asked in
the environment it builds from the finished AST; a #~"public:" block's entries
count as references, so a listed hidden subunit loads on the strength of the
listing alone.

The parser returns the first syntax error found immediately. It does not
attempt to reset and continue.

A parse has two phases: the prelude -- #~"core" and every unit it names, read
as #~".z" whatever kind of file the parse reads -- then the main unit and what
it names. Parses in one process share the prelude through a
#~"zparser.PreludeStore" (#~"Parser.parseShared"): zc checks a project's
#~"project.zd" and then builds each program, #~"zl lint --full" checks each
file it is given and its project, and each parse after the first reads on from
a copy of the tree the first one kept after its prelude. It does so only
when every lookup that found the prelude's files -- each name walked from the
root, or the folder beside a unit's file -- finds the same files on disk in
that parse's own file view; otherwise (a source root holding a unit of the
same name, another stdlib) it reads the prelude itself. #~"zl fmt", which
rewrites files as it goes, and zls, which runs for as long as the editor does,
keep nothing.

#--: Generator Desugaring

A #~"generator" declaration is a class whose #~"call" suspends. Generator
lowering (helpers in #~"src/zgenerator.z", driven by
#~"lowerDeclaredGenerators" in #~"src/ztypecheck/memberaccess.z") prepares each one: it
adds what the suspension transform needs to the declaration the author
wrote, so the rest of the typechecker sees an ordinary class. The lowering
runs early in the typecheck pass, before the definitions are indexed and
before bodies are checked; frozen AST nodes are never mutated.

Pipeline placement:

#code: """
  Lex -> Parse -> Typecheck (lower generators, then resolve + check) -> Emit
"""

#---: What the pass adds

A generator is a DECLARATION, so the class is the author's and the pass adds
only what the suspension transform needs. For

#~"G: generator { f1: T1 ... } as { call: function {in: K} out E is { ... } }"

it produces approximately

#code language=zerolang check=none: """
  G: generator {
      f1: T1                   # the author's fields, untouched
      state: i64               # state-machine cursor, appended
      _fc_N: i64               # one per suspending counted `for`
      _yv_N: K                 # one resume slot per suspension point
      _resume_input: K         # only when `call` declares an input
      <local>_N: T             # one per body local, minted by the checker
  } as {
      call: function {:this in: K} out (option-wrapper-for-E) is {
          # the author's body with its yields intact and the call-built
          # iterables of its suspending `for each` loops hoisted into locals
          # (any other new iterable the checker keeps in a state field) --
          # the state machine is the emitter's job
      }
      create: function {f1: T1.take ...} outx this is {
          return (meta.create state: 0 :f1 ...)
      }
  }
"""

The receiver and the wrapper are injected into the author's #~"call"; the
#~"create" is generated from the #~"is" fields, with each parameter's ownership
following the field it fills. The pass runs BEFORE the definitions are indexed,
because it appends fields to a definition the author wrote and indexing binds a
definition's members where it stands.
Numeric suffix on collision.

#---: Body locals

The body resumes in a later call, so nothing it keeps may live on the C stack
of #~".call". The pass rewrites no local: to the checker a body local is an
ordinary local, with its own variable, scope, moves and invalidation, and only
where it is stored differs. Binding a local in a generator's #~"call" body, the
checker mints a field of the instance for it -- one per variable, named
#~"<name>_N" so it collides with nothing else the instance holds, typed by the
variable's own type (#~"bindGenLocal" and #~"mintGenField" in
#~"src/ztypecheck/valuechecks.z") -- and records it in #~"genLocalFieldOf". The emitter
stores into the field where the local is bound and spells every later use of
the local as the field (#~"emitGenLocalBind" in #~"src/zemitterc/stmtblocks.z"). A binding
frees what the field held before -- a loop body binds its locals afresh on
every pass -- and the instance's destructor frees what the field holds last.

Two kinds of local are not kept. A #~"yield" value (#~"a: yield e") lives in
its resume slot. A borrow of a place -- another local, a field, an element --
is a pointer into it, stays a C local, and may not be in scope at a yield
(#~"reportBorrowsAtYield"). A view is a value like any other, and is kept. The
payload a suspending loop binds, the iterable it constructs and the owned value
of a suspending #~"with" are the checker's fields as well
(#~"mintForSpillFields", #~"withValueField").

#---: State-machine codegen

The emitter (#~"src/zemitterc/forloops.z") routes any function whose
#~"synthOrigin == \"generator-call\"" through a dedicated state-machine
path. Helpers #~"emitGenPrologue", #~"emitYieldStmt", and
#~"emitGenEpilogue" assign one state number per yield
(entry = 0, terminal = -1) and emit:

#code: """
  switch (this->state) {
      case 0:  goto L_entry;
      case 1:  goto L_resume_1;
      ...
      default: goto L_done;
  }
  L_entry:
      /* original code up to first yield */
      this->state = 1;
      return OPTION_SOME(<yield expr>);
  L_resume_1:
      /* if takes != null: bind local from this->_resume_input */
      ...
  L_done:
      this->state = -1;
      return OPTION_NONE;
"""

C17 #~"goto" plus #~"switch" arbitrates cleanly, with no compiler
extension.
The yield-state numbers are pre-allocated from the function's yield
count (#~"fnYieldCount") so the prologue, body fragments, and
epilogue agree.

#---: Termination + bidirectional value flow

A bare #~"return" anywhere in the body or fall-through past the last
yield transitions to the terminal absorbing state. #~"return <value>"
is rejected during desugaring. Code
placed after the last yield runs on the call that crosses into terminal;
it does not run on early drop, where class destruction releases
locks and destroys owned fields via the standard class-lifetime path.

For #~"accepts: K" generators, #~"buildCallMethod" adds a
#~"value: K" parameter; the generated prologue emits an
entry-time store of #~"value" into #~"this->_resume_input"
(destroying any prior owned value first). Expression-form yield
(#~"x: yield v") in the body reads #~"this->_resume_input" after
the resume label so #~"x" binds to the caller's most recent input.
The first reachable yield must be statement form -- the
desugarer rejects expression-form first-yields, reflecting that
the resume-input slot is undefined before the first
#~".call value:" arrives.

#--: Semantic Analyser

The semantic analyser, the typechecker, takes the syntax tree and gives the
model the code generator reads: a type for each node, and the ownership,
lock and narrowing facts of every body (#~"ZTyping"), beside the type
registry. It is demand-driven: it starts from #~"main" and checks only what
a reference reaches. [#> to=./typechecker.html: The typechecker] owns its
detail:

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to="./typechecker.html#demand-driven-resolution": demand-driven resolution]
    and [#> to="./typechecker.html#resolution-stack-and-cycle-detection": cycle detection];]
  [#*: [#> to="./typechecker.html#monomorphisation": monomorphisation] -- each
    instance of a generic definition checked from its own copy;]
  [#*: [#> to="./typechecker.html#ownership-enforcement": ownership] and
    [#> to="./typechecker.html#lock-checking": lock checking];]
  [#*: [#> to="./typechecker.html#numeric-literal-coercion": numeric literal coercion]
    and [#> to="./typechecker.html#type-narrowing": type narrowing];]
  [#*: [#> to="./typechecker.html#synthesis-toolkit": the nodes it synthesises].]

The ownership rules as a program meets them are in
[#> to=./ownership.html: Ownership].

#--: Code Generator

The code generator (C emitter) walks the fully typed AST and emits C source
code. A C compiler -- gcc, clang, tcc, mingw-w64 or MSVC -- compiles the
output into a native executable.

The emitter targets the #b:"C17" language standard
(#~"-std=c17 -Wall -Wextra"). The one thing beyond C17 the output may need
is the type of an expression, for a temporary whose type the emitter has no
C text for: the #~"Z_TYPEOF" macro spells it #~"typeof" under C23 and
#~"__typeof__" otherwise, which each of those compilers takes in C17 mode.

The emitter produces several output sections in order: struct/type definitions,
forward declarations, function typedefs, function bodies, and a #~"main()"
entry point. Each section is collected separately and assembled at the end.

#---: The files of the emitter

The emitter is one unit, #~"zemitterc", written in fifteen files: the
unit's own file and the fourteen implementation subunits of the folder
#~"src/zemitterc/". A subunit reads the unit's definitions by their bare
names; the unit's other files name it as #~"file.name". #~"emitC", the one
entry point, is in #~"emitplan.z" and listed in the #~"public:" block of
#~"src/zemitterc.z" as #~"emitC: emitplan.emitC".

#table:
  [#tr: [#th: File] [#th: What it holds]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"src/zemitterc.z"] [#td: the #~"public:" block, #~"Program", #~"Ctx" and its parts, the shared types and the helpers every part calls]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"emitplan.z"] [#td: what is written and in what order: units, functions, reach, C names]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"nativepieces.z"] [#td: loading the native C pieces: files, conditions, checked and served text]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"nativerows.z"] [#td: the native rows and templates a program uses: keys, holes, fills]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"types.z"] [#td: type definitions, destroy functions, and what family a type is]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"consts.z"] [#td: constants, literals and static data as C text]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"stmtblocks.z"] [#td: statements, blocks, scope ends and returns]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"values.z"] [#td: value expressions: control values, moves and zeroing, operands, reads]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"callorder.z"] [#td: evaluation order of a call's parts, and the call forms that use it]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"argforms.z"] [#td: how one argument is passed: by value, pointer, box, label or position]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"memberreads.z"] [#td: member reads and the methods of strings and collections]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"parttexts.z"] [#td: interpolation parts, and the equality and hash functions of a type]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"forloops.z"] [#td: #~"for" forms, ranges, and a generator's state machine]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"narrowfacts.z"] [#td: narrowing facts: arm seeds, variable entries, aliases]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"localnames.z"] [#td: a local's C name and what a binding refers to]]

Two values travel through it. #~"prog: Program" is the program as the
checker left it -- the tree, the type registry and the checker's tables,
read-only -- and every function that reads the program takes it.
#~"ctx: Ctx" is what the emission has written and decided so far; its parts
are records of their own (#~"ctx.pieces", #~"ctx.reach", #~"ctx.stmt",
#~"ctx.gen", #~"ctx.order", #~"ctx.known", #~"ctx.narrow",
#~"ctx.literals", #~"ctx.typeDefs", #~"ctx.plan"), and a function that
touches only one part takes that part in place of #~"ctx".

Reading order: #~"emitC" (#~"emitplan.z") for the order things are
written in; #~"stmtblocks.z" and #~"values.z" for a function body;
#~"callorder.z" and #~"argforms.z" for a call; the rest by concern.

#---: The backend contract

The front end -- lexer, parser, type checker, generator lowering -- knows no
backend. It hands one over a checked program: the AST, the type registry and
the #~"ZTyping" model, every value it can answer already a constant of the
program, and the set of units the program reaches (#~"ZTyping.unitsReached").
What it decides holds for every backend: whether a program is legal, the
language's own sizes (a union's slot is 8 bytes on every target). It knows
nothing of the target: the drivers resolve
the triple (#~"src/ztarget.z") and write what the build is for into the
generated #~"z" unit (#~"z.build.os", #~"z.build.arch"), whose values are
ordinary constants the checker folds like any other. It never reads a
toolchain. The test runner's pins kind holds its code to no C spelling, no
triple parse and no read of the target.

Two facts the front end keeps are there for the C backend, and stay in the
front end because the walk that finds them is its own:

#ul:
  [#*: the generator lowering leaves a suspending loop's counted range
    (#~"F.upto to: T", #~"F.downto to: T") in its header rather than hoisting
    it (#~"countedRangeHeader", #~"condIsTrivial" in #~"src/zgenerator.z"):
    the C backend writes that header as a counted C #~"for" with no range
    object, and a hoisted header would build one;]
  [#*: the checker records an untyped numeric constant that lands in an
    #~"i128", #~"u128" or #~"f16" destination with that destination
    (#~"ZTyping.helperScalarTarget"): C reads such a constant as a 64-bit
    integer or a double, and where the C compiler lacks the destination's type
    nothing converts it implicitly, so the emitter spells it as the
    destination's value.]

Each is a fact about the program, which another backend is free to ignore.

A backend supplies the rest:

#ul:
  [#*: an implementation of every #~"is native" declaration, refusing at the
    declaration one it has none for -- the C
    backend's are the rows of each unit's #~"nativec.zd", which #~"zc"
    carries in itself (see [#> to=./native_units.html: Native Units]);]
  [#*: its representation of each type, holding every value the language's
    rules admit -- the C backend gives a type the C compiler lacks (#~"i128",
    #~"u128", #~"f16") a struct and helper functions, and the C preprocessor,
    not #~"zc", picks between that and the compiler's own type;]
  [#*: what a reached unit needs of its toolchain, and the refusal of a
    toolchain that lacks it, by name and before anything is compiled -- the C
    backend's are a unit's links, sources and refusals in its
    #~"nativec.zd": #~"system.quadfloat" needs #~"__float128", which tcc and
    MSVC have none of and only x86-64 targets have, and links libquadmath;
    #~"sqlite" needs its vendored C source compiled (a #~"source" with its
    #~"defines"), which the driver builds into its object cache with the
    compiler that links.]

#---: Runtime-backed stdlib units

Five stdlib units -- #~"system", #~"core", #~"collections", #~"io",
#~"os" -- are #_*"runtime-backed": their types (String, File,
the collections, the numerics) and their #~"is native" functions are
implemented in C by the rows of each unit's #~"nativec.zd"
(#~"lib/system/<unit>/nativec.zd"), which #~"zc" carries in itself. The
emitter never generates structs or type-method bodies for them;
instead native FREE functions are #_*"declaration-driven": one
emitter (#~"emitNativeFreeFnCall") derives the C symbol
#~"z_<unit>_<name>" from the resolved declaration and lowers every
argument from the declared parameter types (StringView / StringLike
params take a by-value view, class and borrowed-List params pass by
pointer, #~"take" params pass the owned value, scalars pass
verbatim) -- for both the dotted (#~"io.remove") and bare same-unit
call shapes. The C implementation is the fragment of the function's row,
keyed by its name, in its unit's #~"nativec.zd". Adding a native to a
convention unit is a declaration plus a row -- no emitter edits; a new
native-bearing unit is a #~"nativec.zd" of its own and a place in the
foundation's #~"order" (#~"system/nativec.zd"), which is the order
#~"emitRuntimeUnit" walks. The #~"make native-guard" ratchet (run in
#~"make ci", #~"zc natives check rows") asserts that each #~"is native"
declaration of a unit with rows has a row, that every hole a text spells
names a declared type, arm or member, and that the numeric conversions'
rows are the ones the numeric rule gives.

A class's #_*"native methods" are rows of their own, keyed
#~"<Class>.<method>" in its unit's file, whose fragment defines
#~"<Class>_<method>" called with the receiver's address and each
argument by one rule decided by its type (a value view by value, an
owned collection by address); a protocol slot takes the same, so a
native method fills a vtable as it is. An entry of the unit's #~"classes"
(with #~"live" and #~"destroy") makes a class the runtime's: its struct
is written ahead of the fragments that read it, once the class is live
or used, and #~"destroy" is the C that releases what the declaration
cannot show (an fd, a database handle). #~"io.File",
#~"net.Listener" / #~"net.Conn" and #~"sqlite"'s classes are all this
one mechanism. A row names its unit and type by spelling, and applies
only to a declaration that is native or declares something native, so a
user unit that shadows a stdlib one by name gets none of its rows.

#_*"Bodied free functions" in these units are ordinary emitted
zerolang (#~"io.removeTree" is the living example). They are
demand-gated end to end, as every unit's are: resolved when a reference
reaches them and walked with every other resolved body; the emitter
correspondingly skips undemanded bodies in #~"emitFnsIn" and the
literal pool, so an unused stdlib body cannot drag runtime fragments
or unmaterialized monos into unrelated programs. Sibling
natives may be called bare or dotted -- both shapes resolve through
the declaration-driven native emitter -- and #~".take" references to
bodied stdlib functions work like any function reference (natives
themselves have no reference form; they are call-only).
A #_*"type method" in these units may be a #~".z" body, and
#~"u8.times" and its seven siblings are: they construct an #~"intrange"
and hand it back, and they emit and run like any other method.

A method body is demanded per MEMBER, as a free function is: resolving a
type walks only the bodied members the program uses
(#~"bodiedMemberDeferred"), asked from #~"usedMemberDecl". A walk MINTS
whatever the body constructs, so walking every member of every type a
program touches would land those types in its C -- every program touches
the scalars, so a bodied #~"u8.times" would be walked and emitted by every
compile. Code that is never reached must parse; it is not
typechecked, and #~"zl lint --full" and #~"--eager" check it.

A use is any reference that reaches the member, not only a dotted read: an
operator, a construction (a written #~"create"), a call or #~"for" over a
callable object (its #~"call"), a list's #~"contains", #~"=="
and #~"sort" (the element's #~"==" or #~"compare"), a Map or Set key (its
#~"hash" and #~"=="), the generated #~"==" every value type is emitted with
(each part's own), and a function field naming a sibling method. A
implementation uses every member its spec names, on the implementer and on each
link of a typedef standing in for it: the vtable wrapper is emitted from
the implementation row, not from a use site. A use of a generic instance's
member uses the template's too, whose walk reports on the body, and each
instance -- of a generic type or of a generic unit -- walks and emits only the
members used on it (#~"instanceMemberOwed"), revisited when a later use
reaches another. A use found
after the owner walked has its member walked then (#~"walkLateUsedBodies").
#~"forceOwnerMember" is asked BELOW the gate, because forcing a member mints
its return type and would drag the types in regardless. The emitter writes a
method body only when the checker walked it. #~"--eager" walks everything,
which is the point of it.

With the gate, a bodied #~"times" on all eight scalars costs ONE line of
C in a program that never iterates.

#---: C Name Generation

C names are the emitter's business -- the type checker composes none. At
the start of #~"emitC" a single pass over the type registry fills a
#~"tid"-indexed table on #~"Ctx.cnames", and every emission site reads it
back through #~"ctxCname" (a hole minted by #~"allocType" gets #~""). The
table is built once from a frozen model, so a name cannot go stale, and one
function composes every name, so a definition and its references cannot
disagree.

The composer is #~"cnameOf" (#~"src/zemitterc/emitplan.z"). Outside the table builder only two
sites call it, both naming a symbol that no registered type owns: the
flattened #~"{base}.{member}" of a dotted member access, and an #~"as"-block
constant's #~"{type}.{label}". Variable names go through
#~"mangleVarName" (#~"src/zemitterc/localnames.z"; C-keyword mangling of the emitted identifier
only). The test runner's pins kind (run by #~"make test") pins the
emitter's by-name resolution sites at their current floors and caps the
#~"z_t{id}" spelling at the composer itself (src/ztestrunner/expected.z
holds each pin).

#---: Id-based naming

A type's (and a non-native function's) C name #b:"is" its monotonic id under
the #~"z_" namespace prefix: the type #~"point" with id 525 becomes
#~"z_t525_t", its #b:"add" method (a function type of its own, id 527)
#~"z_t527". Derived symbols append a literal suffix to that base --
#~"z_t525_create", #~"z_t525_destroy", #~"z_t525_meta_create". Because the
id is unique by construction, names cannot collide: there is no dedup set
and no on-collision suffix.

#~"--readable-names" switches the table to #~"cnameOf", which appends the
source name to the same base -- #~"z_t525_point_t", #~"z_t527_point_add".
Nothing else changes, because the id is the unique part in both schemes and
the name is pure decoration; it costs about 16% of the emitted file. A
method's owner name comes from #~"ZType.ownerTid", the id #~"newType" is
handed at mint: the registry stores the id, never a composed string.

Three kinds of symbol keep a source name in #_*"both" schemes. The
hand-written runtime ABI -- native functions (#~"z_io_open",
#~"z_String_create", ...) and the C entry point #~"z_main" -- must match C
written by hand. An #~"as"-block constant is named
#~"z_t{owner_id}_{type}_{label}", because several constants share their
owner's id and only the label separates them. And a struct member has no id
space at all: its namespace is the struct, so nothing but a C reserved word
can collide with it, and a field named #~"default" would emit
#~"int64_t default;". Every spelling of a member therefore goes through
#~"memberCName" -- the declaration, the reads and writes, the constructor
parameters, the destructor, the generated #~"_eq", and the four spellings of
an interface vtable slot that #~"cSlotName" keeps in step. They must agree:
a mangler applied at the declaration and not at a read emits
#~"b.v_default" against a field named #~"default".

Local variables have an id space of their own: a binding the type checker
gave a variable id emits #~"z_v{id}", and only a site no id reached falls
back to the source name through #~"mangleVarName", which dodges the C
reserved words. #~"--readable-names" puts the source name in front of the
id (#~"n_5"). The id is still what makes the name unique: a source name is
unique only inside the scope that binds it, while a bare block is flattened
into its enclosing C block, so two sibling scopes binding #~"n" emit
#~"n_5" and #~"n_9". It also keeps a local clear of the macros system
headers define -- #~"near" and #~"FAR" in #~"windows.h", #~"errno" and
#~"stdin" in the C runtime -- which no list of reserved words could cover.

The native C (the rows, templates and fixed files of the #~"nativec.zd"
files) is written with the #b:"canonical" type spelling #~"z_@Name@": each
#~"@Name@" is a hole, and each #~"@@KEY@@" in a template a key its writer
binds. Every native text -- a row's body, a template, a fragment, a fixed
file -- is parsed once, when it is first read
(#~"RtTexts"): cut into literal stretches, holes and keys, with its member
sections or row pieces found. A fill walks the stretches once, binding every
hole to the id-named cname the registry gives that canon (collection monos,
#~"z_CpIter", parse-result monos included) and every key to the text its
writer bound, and writes only the sections and pieces the program keeps; a
value put in a hole is never searched again. A hole naming nothing the loader
can resolve is fatal. Pure-C helpers (#~"z_panic", #~"z_xmalloc",
#~"z_siphash_*") spell no hole and are untouched.

#---: Type Emission

Each zerolang type maps to a C representation:

#ul:
  [#*: Records → C structs (stack-allocated value types)]
  [#*: Classes → C structs, stack-allocated and passed by pointer; single-owner, with a generated destructor when they own heap-backed fields]
  [#*: Unions → tagged struct (tag + void* to data), stack-allocated; the active subtype's data is heap-allocated behind the void*]
  [#*: Variants → tagged struct with inline data union (stack-allocated)]
  [#*: Protocols → vtable struct + instance struct (dynamic dispatch via function pointers)]
  [#*: Facets → vtable + inline data union (value-type dynamic dispatch)]
  [#*: Enums → integer constants with a tag enum type]

#---: Memory Management

The emitter implements ownership-based memory management without a garbage
collector.

String data buffers are heap-allocated and freed via #~"z_String_free()".
Class instances with owned fields are freed via generated
#~"z_<type>_destroy()" functions that recursively free those fields.

The emitter tracks variables that need cleanup in a per-function scope. At
scope exit (function return or fall-through), all owned variables are destroyed
in reverse declaration order. Each type carries #~"needsFieldCleanup",
#~"destructorName", and #~"isHeapAllocated" metadata set by the type
checker, so the emitter delegates cleanup decisions to the type system.

Temporary values (intermediate results within a statement) are tracked
separately and freed at the end of each statement. When a temporary is
assigned to a named variable, ownership transfers and the temporary is removed
from the free list.

A value that has no address -- a call's result handed to a parameter that takes
a pointer, a receiver read through, a value read twice -- is bound to a
statement temporary first (#~"hoistForAddress" in #~"src/zemitterc/argforms.z"). Every
such temporary owes a destroy by one rule: exactly when the checker's
temporary for that value node still owns what it holds where it dies
(#~"scopeOwnsTemp", read from the checker's per-scope temporary record). A
borrow the call's result shares, a value moved out on the way, and a pointer
into storage something else owns owe none. The emitter never decides ownership
from the shape of what it hoists; a hoist with no node to ask about holds a
value that owns nothing, and says why where it is written.

A value written where a protocol or a facet is expected, without a projection
of its own, stands in for the spec. The type checker records each stand-in that
holds (#~"standInOf", keyed by the value's node, with the spec and whether the
place owns it), and the emitter boxes that node as the spec wherever a slot
spells it -- an argument of any call form, an operator operand, a field, an
element, a reassigned value, a return (#~"implementationArgText"): the box a
#~"P.create from: x" of the same value builds, owned where the place owns a
protocol value, borrowed otherwise.

The #~".take" operation transfers ownership from one variable to another,
nullifying the source to prevent double-free.

#---: Unrecoverable Errors

All runtime-terminal exits route through a single helper emitted in
the stdlib preamble of every program:

#code language=c: """
  static _Noreturn void z_panic(const char* msg) {
      fprintf(stderr, "zpanic: %s\\n", msg);
      exit(1);
  }
"""

User-level #~"panic msg: ..." lowers to a direct call to this
helper. Internal unrecoverable exit sites -- allocation failure in
#~"z_xmalloc" / #~"z_xcalloc" / #~"z_xrealloc", bounds checks on
arrays, lists, listviews and stringviews, numeric cast overflow,
empty-list pop -- all funnel through #~"z_panic" too, so every
termination produces a #~"zpanic: <msg>" line on stderr and an
exit code of #~"1".

Formatted messages (bounds with indices, numeric cast overflow)
use a small stack buffer and #~"snprintf" at the call site to
build the full message before invoking #~"z_panic". The helper
itself takes a plain #~"const char*" -- no variadic #~"<stdarg.h>"
is pulled in. #~"_Noreturn" is C17 and lets GCC's control-flow
analysis eliminate any "control may reach end of non-void
function" diagnostics in callers that panic on every path.

Source location (#~".z" file + line) is intentionally not included
in the #~"zpanic:" output; the panic message text itself is the
post-mortem link back to the emitting site.

#---: Collection Choice

Compiler data structures follow a measured policy. Id-keyed tables (node
stamps, declarations, registries) are #~"IdMap" / #~"IdSet": a stack struct
that costs no allocation while empty, a dense insertion-ordered entry array
with no tombstone and no stored hash, and a slot taken from the key's own
bits. Nothing is ever removed from one, which is what buys all three. A
#~"Map" is what remains for the keys an id space cannot express -- a String,
or a composite record -- and it is the only container of the two that is a
heap object.

#b:"The representation crossing is inside the container, not in the code
that uses it." An IdMap below its small-mode ceiling has no index array at
all and #~"find" scans the dense entries; past it the index appears and the
scan becomes a probe. So a declaration's namespace is one field that answers
both questions asked of it -- #~"children.get" is the lookup and the same
entries in order are the walk -- and no reader carries the crossing as a
branch.

Small, per-instance state that #_*"deletes" (the emitter's narrowing and
alias lists) stays an #b:"assoc-list": at a handful of entries a linear row
scan beats a probe, and an append-only container cannot serve it at all.
Hot String-keyed lookups are avoided outright: resolution goes through
interned ids and two-argument (unit, name) probes rather than composed
"unit.name" key strings.

#---: Stamp-Driven Emission

The emitter is a mechanical typed-AST-to-C pass: it reads what the type
checker stamped and compares by id, never re-resolving names. An atom
reference carries #~"atomVariableId" (a local) or #~"refDecl" (the
declaration it resolved to); a call carries its #~"callKind", which routes
constructions to their owning handler ahead of the probe chain. Member
types are canonicalized at the end of typechecking
(#~"canonicalizeShellChildren"), so a field or parameter type id is the
real defining type, not a name-minted shell. Ownership, mono-origin and
String/StringView tests compare enum values and cached canonical ids
(#~"declOwnershipOfId", #~"originIsTid", #~"ctx.strTid" / #~"svTid").
The residual by-name sites -- generic-template re-emission, the
probe-chain fallbacks -- are counted and pinned by the pins kind.
Internal impossibilities (an unresolvable typedef base, an unspellable
field type) #b:"panic" with a #~"zemitterc:" message instead of
emitting a silent fallback type.

#---: Evaluation Order

A call's arguments and an operator's operands are evaluated in the order they
are written (spec: Call Arguments, Operations), and C leaves both unsequenced.
The emitter keeps the order with #b:"ordered temps": a part is read into an
#~"_oN" temp ahead of the call, in a comma expression, when something evaluated
after it could change what it reads or see what it does. A part that only
#b:"reads" -- no call, assignment, increment, comma or block, as the facts its
producer states say -- needs no temp when every part after it only reads too:
#~"a + b", #~"p.x + p.y" and #~"pair a: y.v b: (y.v + 1)" are plain C. A part
that may write needs one only when a part that is not constant follows it.
#~"h.bump + h.count" keeps a temp for #~"h.bump", and #~"x.v + (addTo c: x d: 1)"
one for #~"x.v". A literal, a folded constant and a plain address never need
one. #~"order_pure_parts" pins each case by running it.

#--: Bootstrapping & self-hosting

The compiler is self-hosted: it is written in zerolang (#~"src/*.z") and
compiles itself. A C toolchain is the only requirement to build and test
it.

#---: The bootstrap chain

The sources are compiled by a #b:"seed": #~"bootstrap/zc.c", a committed,
self-emitted C dump of the compiler. #~"cc bootstrap/zc.c" alone yields a
working #~"zc".

#ul:
  [#*: #~"make bin/zc" builds the seed (#~"out/zc-seed"), then uses it to emit and compile the current #~"src/*.z" into #~"bin/zc", the live self-hosted compiler.]
  [#*: #~"make test-bootstrap" proves the loop converges: the seed builds a compiler, which rebuilds itself to a byte-identical fixpoint (#~"b2 == b3"). Under #~"make ci" it runs that chain for gcc, clang and the vendored tcc, and asserts the three emit byte-identical C — so "any C toolchain" means they agree, not merely that each works.]
  [#*: #~"make bump-seed" regenerates #~"bootstrap/zc.c" from a fresh #~"bin/zc" when the sources have drifted far enough that the seed lags.]

#---: Building and testing

The test runner, the style linter and formatter, and the gate tool are
themselves zerolang programs (#~"src/ztestrunner.z", #~"src/zl.z",
#~"tools/zgate/"), so the whole build-and-test pipeline runs on any platform
with a C compiler. [#> to=./testing.html: Testing] says what each make
target runs and how to add a test.
